keepass2pass
============
A Python script to import KeePass2 XML databases into the `pass` password
store.


Requirements
------------
* Python 3.8 or newer (standard library only)
* `pass` installed and initialised (`pass init <gpg-id>`), with a working
  `gpg` setup


Security note
-------------
The XML export contains ALL your passwords in plain text. Create it on a
RAM-backed filesystem if you can (e.g. `/dev/shm`) and delete it right after
the import. `shred` is not reliable on SSDs and journaling filesystems.

Passwords are handed to `pass` through stdin, never on the command line.


Usage
-----
Make the script executable:

	chmod +x keepass2pass.py

1. Default import (keeps the KeePass root group name as the top folder):

	./keepass2pass.py -f export.xml

2. Custom root folder (REPLACES the KeePass root group name):

	./keepass2pass.py -f export.xml -r "foo"

3. Flat structure (no root folder at all):

	./keepass2pass.py -f export.xml -r ""

4. Preview without touching the store:

	./keepass2pass.py -f export.xml --dry-run

Options:

	-f, --file FILE         KeePass2 XML export (required)
	-r, --root DIR          replace the root group name; "" = flat
	-n, --dry-run           print what would be done, change nothing
	--duplicates MODE       ask (default), merge or split
	--overwrite             overwrite entries that already exist
	--include-recycle-bin   also import the KeePass Recycle Bin

Exit status: 0 on success, 1 if anything failed (unreadable XML, missing or
uninitialised `pass`, failed inserts), 2 on bad arguments.


What is imported
----------------
Each entry becomes one `pass` file:

	<password>
	UserName: ...
	URL: ...
	<custom fields as "Key: value">
	otpauth://...            (an `otp` field with an otpauth:// URI, kept bare
	                          so that pass-otp can use it)
	Tags: ...
	Notes: ...

* The first line is always the password, empty if the entry has none, so
  `pass -c` never copies a metadata line.
* The Recycle Bin is skipped unless `--include-recycle-bin` is given.
* Entries with no content at all are skipped with a warning.
* Titles are sanitised: `/` and control characters become `_`, `.` and `..`
  become `_`, empty titles become `untitled`.


What is NOT imported (a warning is printed for the first two):
--------------------------------------------------------------
* Attachments
* `{REF:...}` field references are copied literally and will not resolve
* Icons, colours, expiry dates and entry history


Existing entries
----------------
Entries that already exist in the store are skipped. Pass `--overwrite` to
replace them. This also makes it safe to re-run the import after a failure.
The store location is taken from `PASSWORD_STORE_DIR` (default
`~/.password-store`).


Duplicate Handling
------------------
Two entries get the same path when they share group and title. With
`--duplicates ask` the script prompts interactively (before anything is
written, so Ctrl+C is safe):

* m  -> Merge this specific duplicate into one file
* s  -> Split this specific duplicate (adds _2, _3, etc.)
* ma -> Merge ALL remaining duplicates automatically
* sa -> Split ALL remaining duplicates automatically

A merged file separates the entries with a `---------` line, and the later
password is prefixed with `Password: `. Without a terminal use
`--duplicates merge` or `--duplicates split`.


Notes
-----
* If your store is a git repository, `pass` makes one commit per entry.
* Paths that differ only by case (`Foo`, `foo`) are reported, because they
  overwrite each other on case-insensitive filesystems.


